            "text": "Combines arrays by naming their axes with letters. A short formula says which axes line up, which are summed away, and which make up the result - so one node can do a dot product, a matrix product, a transpose, a trace, a sum along an axis, or any of these done row by row over a whole batch.\n\nRead a formula like ij,ij->i as: the first array has axes i and j, so does the second; multiply them element by element; keep only i in the result, so sum over j. That is a dot product of each row with the matching row - for 100 joints' velocity vectors against 100 direction vectors, 100 dot products in one step.\n\nA few formulas:\nij,jk->ik    matrix product (np.matmul)\nij,ij->i     dot product of each row with the matching row\ni,j->ij      outer product (np.outer)\nij->ji       transpose\nij->j        sum down each column\nii->         trace (the sum of the diagonal)\nbij,bjk->bik a matrix product for each item of a batch\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.einsum <subscripts>\nDefault: ij,jk->ik. Spaces in the formula are ignored, so ij, jk -> ik is fine.\n\nEXAMPLE:\nnp.einsum ij,ij->i\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe first array. Every input computes and sends - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2, in 3 ...:\nOne more inlet for each further array the formula names (one per comma). Held:\nsend them first.\n\nsubscripts:\nAn option, under the node: the formula. It can be changed to another formula with the same number of arrays - the inlets are made from the formula the node was created with.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nresult:\nThe result, with the axes named after the arrow. With nothing after the arrow, a single number.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: three 2-D vectors in A, three in B. ij,ij->i gives each row's dot product:\n1x2 + 0x3 = 2, 0x4 + 1x5 = 5, 1x1 + 1x1 = 2.\n\nBottom: ij->j has one array and keeps only j: it sums down each column of A, giving 2 2.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.matmul, np.dot, np.inner and np.outer are fixed cases of einsum, and are easier to read when they are what you need. np.transpose and np.sum do the single-array cases."
        },
